name = Themes & Styles
description = An experiment highlighting the importance of appealing visual design.
content =
    As part of this project, I analysed and recreated an existing screen from an app or website,
    then redesigned it in three different UI styles.
    The aim was to explore various design trends and reflect on their impact on different target audiences.
    In addition to the visual implementation, the focus was placed on stylistic research,
    target audience analysis, and a well-founded justification of the design process.


concentration = Focus
concentration-text =
    Screen designs are often categorised into different styles, themes, and trends. These changes primarily affect the visual aspect, not the interactive components of the design.
    To illustrate this, the original screen design was first analysed and reduced to its interactive elements, then transformed into various other themes and styles.
    This version, which contains only the layout and actual content without the full screen design, serves as the base version for the subsequent designs.
    No fundamental layout changes are made — only new visual styles are added.

glass-morphism = Glassmorphism
glass-morphism-sub = Light, depth, and focus — created through transparency and blur for a modern, airy digital aesthetic.
glass-morphism-text =
    It uses transparent, blurred elements with background blur to create a glass-like effect.
    It combines soft colours and smooth edges, resulting in a modern and lightweight design where transparency and depth take centre stage.

glass-morphism-list-1 = transparent surfaces
glass-morphism-list-2 = background blur effect
glass-morphism-list-3 = smooth edges
glass-morphism-list-4 = modern, airy aesthetic

bau = Bauhaus
bau-sub = Geometric, minimalistic, high-contrast — ideal for functional interfaces with a focus on clarity.
bau-text =
    Bauhaus screen design relies on clear, geometric shapes and a reduced colour palette.
    Primary colours and simple shapes such as rectangles and circles dominate the design, balancing function and aesthetics.

bau-list-1 = reduced colour scheme (primary colours)
bau-list-2 = geometric shapes (circles, rectangles)
bau-list-3 = functional focus
